---
// The privacy notice (plan §6; GDPR Art. 13): who is responsible, what the site keeps and why, who else handles
// it, how long it stays, and members' rights. Linked from the footer, /sign-in, About, and Google's consent screen.
// It describes what the code does: change it in the same commit as anything that changes what is kept.
// TODO(after-launch): the cron deletes accounts unused for 24 months, as promised below (docs/engineering.md).
import Base from '../layouts/Base.astro';

export const prerender = false;

const CONTROLLER = 'Juan Mauricio Torres Perez';
const EMAIL = 'juanma71298@gmail.com';
const MEETUP = 'https://www.meetup.com/amsterdam-film-group/';
---

<Base
  title="Privacy · AFiG: Amsterdam Film Group"
  description="What the AFiG: Amsterdam Film Group website keeps about you, why, and how to have it deleted."
>
  <h1>Privacy</h1>
  <p class="lead">
    Reading this site needs no account and sets no cookies. If you sign in, we keep as little as we can, only to run the
    site, and never sell or share it for advertising.
  </p>
  <p class="updated">Last updated 4 October 2026.</p>

  <section aria-labelledby="who">
    <h2 id="who">Who is responsible</h2>
    <p>
      The site is run by volunteers of AFiG: Amsterdam Film Group, a film meetup in Amsterdam. Responsible for your data
      (the controller, in the GDPR's words) is its organizer, {CONTROLLER}, in Amsterdam. Write to
      <a href={`mailto:${EMAIL}`}>{EMAIL}</a> about anything on this page.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section aria-labelledby="visit">
    <h2 id="visit">When you read the site</h2>
    <ul>
      <li>
        <strong>Cloudflare</strong> hosts the site. Like any web server, it receives your IP address and browser details to
        send you the pages and to keep the site safe, and keeps technical logs of requests for up to 7 days to find errors.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Images from other sites:</strong> event pictures load from Meetup's servers and film posters from TMDB's, so
        your browser connects to them, and they see your IP address, as with any image on the web.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Your browser</strong> remembers the theme you pick (Light or Dark), and that you saw the logo's animation in
        this visit. Both stay in your browser; we never receive them.
      </li>
      <li>No analytics, no tracking, no advertising cookies. The site never shows who goes to an event, only how many.</li>
    </ul>
  </section>

  <section aria-labelledby="account">
    <h2 id="account">When you sign in</h2>
    <p>
      You sign in with Google to use the Film finder and Recommend. Google tells us your name, your email address, whether
      Google verified it, and an id for your Google account. We keep those, and when you signed in, only to know it's you.
      We don't keep your Google picture, your password (we never see it) or any access to your Google account. Google knows
      that you signed in to this site; see <a href="https://policies.google.com/privacy">Google's privacy policy</a>.
    </p>
    <p>
      Two cookies keep you signed in: one for up to 90 days, renewed while you use the site, and one that remembers your
      session for 5 minutes so the site stays fast. A third lives a few minutes while you sign in. All are needed for
      signing in, so the site doesn't ask for consent. We don't keep your IP address with your account.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section aria-labelledby="recommend">
    <h2 id="recommend">When you recommend</h2>
    <p>
      What you send in <a href="/recommend">Recommend</a> (a film, a screening link, a note) is kept with your account. The
      group's hosts, a few volunteers, see it with your name and email, so they can reply. Other members never see it.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section aria-labelledby="why">
    <h2 id="why">Why we may keep it</h2>
    <p>
      Signing in is your request to use the parts of the site for members, and we keep only what that needs (GDPR Art.
      6(1)(b)). The technical logs serve our legitimate interest in a site that works and stays safe (Art. 6(1)(f)).
    </p>
  </section>

  <section aria-labelledby="where">
    <h2 id="where">Where it is, and for how long</h2>
    <ul>
      <li>
        Your account and what you send are stored in a Cloudflare database in the EU. Cloudflare handles them only for us,
        under its data processing agreement. Cloudflare and Google are US companies; where data reaches the US, it is
        covered by the EU-US Data Privacy Framework and the EU's standard contractual clauses.
      </li>
      <li>
        Your account stays until you ask us to delete it, or until it hasn't been used for 24 months; then it is deleted
        with everything you sent. You can withdraw a recommendation yourself at any time.
      </li>
    </ul>
  </section>

  <section aria-labelledby="rights">
    <h2 id="rights">Your rights</h2>
    <p>
      You can ask to see what we keep about you, to correct it, to have it deleted, to receive it in a file, or to object
      to how we use it. Write to <a href={`mailto:${EMAIL}`}>{EMAIL}</a>, or message the organizers on
      <a href={MEETUP}>Meetup</a>; we answer within a month. If you think we handle your data wrongly, you can also
      complain to the Dutch data protection authority, the
      <a href="https://autoriteitpersoonsgegevens.nl/">Autoriteit Persoonsgegevens</a>.
    </p>
    <p>Signing in is for people aged 16 or over.</p>
  </section>
</Base>

<style>
  .lead {
    font-size: 18px;
  }

  .updated {
    color: var(--muted);
  }

  section {
    margin-top: 32px;
  }

  h2 {
    margin: 0 0 8px;
    font-family: var(--font-title);
    font-size: 22px;
    font-weight: 400;
  }

  p,
  li {
    line-height: 1.55;
  }

  a {
    color: var(--accent);
  }

  ul {
    padding-left: 20px;
  }

  ul li {
    padding: 2px 0;
  }
</style>
